<!-- SECTION_5_START -->
# 5. KTU 2024 Scheme Examination Question Bank & Topic Recap

## 📘 Part A — Short Answer Questions (3 Marks Each)

### Q1. [KTU University Exam — Dec 2023]
**Differentiate between a constructor and a method in Java. Can a constructor be declared `final` or `static`? Justify.**

**Model Answer:**
A **constructor** is a special member function used to initialize an object, has the **same name as the class**, and **no return type**. It is **invoked automatically** when an object is created using `new`.

A **method** is a general member function that performs a specific action, has **any valid name**, **must specify a return type** (or `void`), and is **invoked explicitly** using an object reference.

A constructor **cannot** be declared `final` (because constructors are never inherited, so `final` is meaningless) and **cannot** be `static` (because static members belong to the class, but constructors must operate on instance data of a specific object).

*(3 marks — concise definition, clear distinction, valid justification)*

### Q2. [KTU University Exam — June 2024]
**What is a copy constructor? Write a Java program to demonstrate it.**

**Model Answer:**
A **copy constructor** is a constructor that takes an object of the **same class** as a parameter and initializes a new object with the values of the existing object.

```java
class Point {
    int x, y;

    Point(int a, int b) { x = a; y = b; }

    Point(Point p) {      // Copy constructor
        x = p.x;
        y = p.y;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("x = " + x + ", y = " + y);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Point p1 = new Point(10, 20);
        Point p2 = new Point(p1);
        p2.display();
    }
}
```

*(3 marks — definition 1 mark, code 2 marks)*

---

## 📗 Part B — Long Answer Questions (14 Marks Each — Internal Choice)

### ❓ Question A (14 Marks) [KTU University Exam — Dec 2023]

**(a)** Explain the concept of **constructor overloading** in Java with a suitable example. *(7 marks)*

**(b)** Write a Java program to demonstrate the use of the **`this` keyword** to refer to:
- (i) current instance variables
- (ii) current object
- (iii) constructor chaining using `this()`

Provide the complete program and explain its output. *(7 marks)*

---

### ✅ Model Solution for Question A

#### (a) Constructor Overloading — Explanation (7 marks)

**Definition:** Constructor overloading is the technique of defining **multiple constructors** within the same class, each having a **different parameter list** (varying in number, type, or order of parameters). It allows objects to be initialized in **different ways** depending on the data available at the time of creation.

**Why is it useful?**
- It provides **flexibility** — sometimes we have all data, sometimes only partial.
- It improves **readability** and supports **polymorphism** at object creation time.
- The compiler resolves which constructor to call at **compile time** based on the arguments passed (a form of **static/compile-time polymorphism**).

**Example Program:**
```java
class Employee {
    String name;
    int id;
    double salary;

    // Constructor 1: No-arg
    Employee() {
        name = "Unknown";
        id = 0;
        salary = 0.0;
    }

    // Constructor 2: Two parameters
    Employee(String n, int i) {
        name = n;
        id = i;
        salary = 25000.0;  // default salary
    }

    // Constructor 3: Three parameters
    Employee(String n, int i, double s) {
        name = n;
        id = i;
        salary = s;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println(id + " | " + name + " | ₹" + salary);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Employee e1 = new Employee();
        Employee e2 = new Employee("Arun", 101);
        Employee e3 = new Employee("Meera", 102, 75000.0);

        e1.display();
        e2.display();
        e3.display();
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
0 | Unknown | ₹0.0
101 | Arun | ₹25000.0
102 | Meera | ₹75000.0
```

**Resolution:** The compiler matches the arguments in `new Employee(...)` with the parameter signatures of the three constructors, choosing the best match. This is resolved at **compile time** — hence, constructor overloading is a form of **static polymorphism**.

---

#### (b) `this` Keyword — Demonstration (7 marks)

The `this` keyword in Java is a **reference variable** that refers to the **current object** — the object whose method or constructor is being executed.

**Three uses demonstrated:**

```java
class Student {
    int rollNo;
    String name;
    String college;

    // Use (iii): Constructor chaining using this()
    Student() {
        this(0, "Not Assigned", "KTU");   // calls 3-arg constructor
    }

    Student(int rollNo, String name) {
        this.rollNo = rollNo;              // (i) refers to current instance var
        this.name = name;
        this.college = "KTU";
    }

    Student(int rollNo, String name, String college) {
        this.rollNo = rollNo;              // (i)
        this.name = name;                  // (i)
        this.college = college;
    }

    void print(Student obj) {
        System.out.println("Roll: " + obj.rollNo);
    }

    void show() {
        print(this);                       // (ii) 'this' refers to current object
        System.out.println("College: " + college);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student();
        Student s2 = new Student(7, "Anu");
        Student s3 = new Student(8, "Rahul", "CET");

        s1.show();
        s2.show();
        s3.show();
    }
}
```

**Explanation of three uses:**
1. **`this.rollNo = rollNo;`** — Disambiguates between the **instance variable** and the **local parameter** when both share the same name.
2. **`print(this);`** — Passes the **current object** as an argument to another method.
3. **`this(0, "Not Assigned", "KTU");`** — Invokes another constructor in the same class (constructor chaining). **Must be the first statement** of the constructor.

---

### ❓ Question B (14 Marks) — ALTERNATIVE CHOICE [KTU University Exam — June 2024]

**(a)** Explain the **default constructor** and **parameterized constructor** with examples. What happens if a class defines only a parameterized constructor and you try to create an object with no arguments? *(7 marks)*

**(b)** Write a Java program to define a class `Rectangle` with length and breadth. Demonstrate:
- A **default constructor** that sets dimensions to 1.
- A **parameterized constructor** that accepts dimensions.
- A **copy constructor** that creates a new rectangle from an existing one.
- A method `area()` that returns the area.

*(7 marks)*

---

### ✅ Model Solution for Question B

#### (a) Default vs Parameterized Constructor (7 marks)

**Default Constructor:** A constructor that accepts **no arguments** is called a *default constructor*. It initializes all instance variables to their **default values** (numeric → 0, boolean → false, object refs → null). If the programmer writes **no constructor at all**, the compiler **automatically provides a public default constructor**.

```java
class Demo {
    int x;
    Demo() {                 // Explicit default constructor
        x = 100;
    }
}
```

**Parameterized Constructor:** A constructor that accepts **one or more parameters** is called a *parameterized constructor*. It allows the caller to pass initial values at the time of object creation.

```java
class Demo {
    int x;
    Demo(int val) {          // Parameterized
        x = val;
    }
}
```

**Critical Concept — If only a parameterized constructor exists:**
```java
class Demo {
    int x;
    Demo(int val) { x = val; }   // Only parameterized
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
    Demo d = new Demo();   // ❌ COMPILE-TIME ERROR
}
```
The compiler will throw: **"constructor Demo in class Demo cannot be applied to given types; required: int, found: no arguments"**.

**Why?** The compiler **only** auto-generates a default constructor if the programmer has **not written any constructor at all**. The moment you write a parameterized constructor, the auto-default is **removed**. The programmer must then **explicitly write** a no-arg constructor if needed.

---

#### (b) Rectangle Class — Full Program (7 marks)

```java
class Rectangle {
    double length, breadth;

    // Default constructor
    Rectangle() {
        length = 1.0;
        breadth = 1.0;
        System.out.println("Default constructor called.");
    }

    // Parameterized constructor
    Rectangle(double l, double b) {
        length = l;
        breadth = b;
        System.out.println("Parameterized constructor called.");
    }

    // Copy constructor
    Rectangle(Rectangle r) {
        length = r.length;
        breadth = r.breadth;
        System.out.println("Copy constructor called.");
    }

    double area() {
        return length * breadth;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Rectangle r1 = new Rectangle();
        Rectangle r2 = new Rectangle(5.0, 3.0);
        Rectangle r3 = new Rectangle(r2);

        System.out.println("Area r1: " + r1.area());
        System.out.println("Area r2: " + r2.area());
        System.out.println("Area r3: " + r3.area());
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Default constructor called.
Parameterized constructor called.
Copy constructor called.
Area r1: 1.0
Area r2: 15.0
Area r3: 15.0
```

> [!WARNING]
> **🚨 KTU Examiner's Valuation Warning — Common Pitfalls:**
> 1. **Forgetting the default constructor after adding a parameterized one** — students often write `new ClassName()` expecting it to work, leading to compile-time errors. **Loss: 2 marks.**
> 2. **Using a return type with a constructor** (e.g., `void Rectangle()`) — this turns it into a regular method, not a constructor. **Loss: 2 marks.**
> 3. **Placing `this()` call NOT as the first statement** — the compiler will reject it. **Loss: 1 mark.**
> 4. **Confusing `this` (current object) with `this()` (constructor call)** — they look similar but are syntactically different. **Loss: 1 mark.**
> 5. **In copy constructors, forgetting to handle deep copy of object references** — for fields like `String[]`, a shallow copy can cause aliasing bugs. (Mentioned in 14-mark answers, partial credit can still be awarded.)

---

## 🔁 Topic Recap & Important Things to Remember

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **High-Yield Rapid Revision Checklist — Constructors**

- ⭐ **Constructor = Class Name + No Return Type** — the two golden identifiers.
- ⭐ **Auto-Default Constructor Rule:** provided **only** if the class has **zero** written constructors.
- ⭐ **Constructor Overloading** = multiple constructors, different parameter lists, compile-time polymorphism.
- ⭐ **Copy Constructor** = takes an object of the same class; built-in in C++, manual in Java.
- ⭐ **`this` Keyword** has **three** uses: instance-variable disambiguation, passing current object, and constructor chaining.
- ⭐ **`this()` call** must be the **first statement** in a constructor body.
- ⭐ **Constructors are NOT inherited** in Java (they belong to their own class only).
- ⭐ **Constructors cannot be** `abstract`, `static`, `final`, or `synchronized` in Java.
- ⭐ **Private constructors** are used to implement the **Singleton Design Pattern**.
- ⭐ **Destructors:** C++ has `~ClassName()`; Java has **Garbage Collection** (`finalize()` was deprecated in Java 9).
- ⭐ **Order of execution during object creation:** Static block → Instance initializer block → Constructor body.
- ⭐ **Memory:** Constructor allocates the object on the **heap**; the reference variable sits on the **stack**.
<!-- SECTION_5_END -->

