## 📌 Topic Recap & Important Things to Remember

### Singleton Pattern
- ✅ Singleton is a **Creational** design pattern.
- ✅ Three pillars: **Private constructor + Private static instance + Public static accessor**.
- ✅ Bill Pugh solution is the **recommended best practice** in Java.
- ✅ `volatile` is **mandatory** in Double-Checked Locking.
- ✅ Eager initialization wastes memory if the instance is never used.
- ✅ Real-world examples: `java.lang.Runtime`, Logger classes, DB Connection Pool, Caches.
- ❌ Don't confuse Singleton with **static class** — static classes cannot be passed as parameters or implement interfaces.

### Adapter Pattern
- ✅ Adapter is a **Structural** design pattern — often called a **Wrapper**.
- ✅ Java prefers **Object Adapter** (composition) over **Class Adapter** (inheritance).
- ✅ Four GoF participants: **Target, Client, Adaptee, Adapter**.
- ✅ Real-world Java examples: `InputStreamReader`, `OutputStreamWriter`, JDBC `DriverManager`, Spring `HandlerAdapter`.
- ✅ Adapter does **not add new functionality** — it only **translates interfaces**.
- ❌ Don't confuse Adapter with **Facade** — Facade simplifies a complex subsystem, while Adapter reconciles **incompatible interfaces**.

### Venn-style Quick Summary
- **Singleton** = "**One** instance, globally accessible."
- **Adapter** = "**Bridge** between incompatible interfaces."
- Both are GoF patterns but serve **fundamentally different purposes** (creation vs. structure).
- In KTU 2024 ESE, expect **either** Singleton **or** Adapter as a 14-mark question in Module 3 (Design Patterns).
