# 📘 SECTION 2: Deep Theoretical Analysis & KTU High-Yield Reference

## 2.1 The Delegation Event Model — The "Why" Behind Swing Events

Before Java 1.1, the **Inheritance Event Model** was used (components inherited and overrode `handleEvent()`). It was inefficient because subclasses had to contain handling code even if not needed. Java 1.1 introduced the **Delegation Event Model**, which **decouples** event generation from event processing.

### 2.1.1 Three-Step Operational Flow

1. **Registration (Subscription Phase):**
   - The listener object is registered with the event source using a method like `addActionListener()`, `addMouseListener()`, etc.
   - Source internally stores the listener in a listener list (often an `EventListener` vector).

2. **Event Generation (Trigger Phase):**
   - User interacts (e.g., clicks a button).
   - Source creates an `EventObject` subclass (e.g., `new ActionEvent(this, ...)`) and invokes the appropriate listener method (`actionPerformed()`).

3. **Event Handling (Response Phase):**
   - The listener's callback method executes.
   - The Event Dispatch Thread (EDT) ensures thread-safety.

---

## 2.2 Event Source Methods: Registration & Removal

Each event category has specific registration methods. Here is the **KTU Cheat Sheet**:

| Event Category | Registration Method | Removal Method | Listener Interface |
|----------------|---------------------|----------------|--------------------|
| **Action** (buttons, menus) | `addActionListener(L)` | `removeActionListener(L)` | `ActionListener` |
| **Mouse** (clicks, moves) | `addMouseListener(L)` | `removeMouseListener(L)` | `MouseListener` |
| **Mouse Motion** (drag, move) | `addMouseMotionListener(L)` | `removeMouseMotionListener(L)` | `MouseMotionListener` |
| **Key** (keyboard) | `addKeyListener(L)` | `removeKeyListener(L)` | `KeyListener` |
| **Window** (open, close) | `addWindowListener(L)` | `removeWindowListener(L)` | `WindowListener` |
| **Focus** | `addFocusListener(L)` | `removeFocusListener(L)` | `FocusListener` |
| **Item** (checkbox, list) | `addItemListener(L)` | `removeItemListener(L)` | `ItemListener` |

> [!NOTE]
> **Key Naming Convention:** KTU examiners love asking "Which method is used to register a mouse listener?" — the answer is always `addMouseListener()`.

---

## 2.3 Detailed Listener Interfaces & Their Abstract Methods

This is the **most high-yield table for KTU exams** — memorize it.

### 2.3.1 `ActionListener` (1 method)

```java
public interface ActionListener extends EventListener {
    void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e);
}
```

### 2.3.2 `MouseListener` (5 methods)

```java
public interface MouseListener extends EventListener {
    void mouseClicked(MouseEvent e);
    void mousePressed(MouseEvent e);
    void mouseReleased(MouseEvent e);
    void mouseEntered(MouseEvent e);
    void mouseExited(MouseEvent e);
}
```

### 2.3.3 `MouseMotionListener` (2 methods)

```java
public interface MouseMotionListener extends EventListener {
    void mouseDragged(MouseEvent e);
    void mouseMoved(MouseEvent e);
}
```

### 2.3.4 `KeyListener` (3 methods)

```java
public interface KeyListener extends EventListener {
    void keyPressed(KeyEvent e);
    void keyReleased(KeyEvent e);
    void keyTyped(KeyEvent e);
}
```

### 2.3.5 `WindowListener` (7 methods)

```java
public interface WindowListener extends EventListener {
    void windowOpened(WindowEvent e);
    void windowClosing(WindowEvent e);
    void windowClosed(WindowEvent e);
    void windowIconified(WindowEvent e);
    void windowDeiconified(WindowEvent e);
    void windowActivated(WindowEvent e);
    void windowDeactivated(WindowEvent e);
}
```

### 2.3.6 `ItemListener` (1 method)

```java
public interface ItemListener extends EventListener {
    void itemStateChanged(ItemEvent e);
}
```

---

## 2.4 Adapter Classes — Solving the "Empty Method" Problem

### 2.4.1 The Problem

If you implement `MouseListener`, Java **forces** you to provide all 5 methods, even if you only care about `mouseClicked()`. This creates boilerplate code.

### 2.4.2 The Solution: Adapters

Java provides **adapter classes** that implement the listener interface with **empty (no-op) methods**. You override only what you need.

| Interface | Adapter Class | Empty Methods Provided |
|-----------|---------------|------------------------|
| `MouseListener` | `MouseAdapter` | 5 |
| `MouseMotionListener` | `MouseMotionAdapter` | 2 |
| `KeyListener` | `KeyAdapter` | 3 |
| `WindowListener` | `WindowAdapter` | 7 |
| `FocusListener` | `FocusAdapter` | 2 |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **No `ActionAdapter` or `ItemAdapter` exist** because their interfaces have only one method, which you would implement directly.

---

## 2.5 Four Ways to Implement Event Handling in Swings

This is a **favorite KTU question** — "List different ways to handle events in Java."

| # | Method | When to Use |
|---|--------|-------------|
| **1** | **Implementing Listener Interface** (separate class) | When listener logic is reused across components. |
| **2** | **Extending Adapter Class** (separate class) | When you need only 1–2 methods of a multi-method interface. |
| **3** | **Anonymous Inner Class** | Quick, one-off handlers (most common in modern Swing). |
| **4** | **Lambda Expression** (Java 8+) | Concise syntax for single-method interfaces (functional interfaces like `ActionListener`). |

---

## 2.6 Key Event Methods (Frequently Tested)

For `MouseEvent` and `KeyEvent`, you must know these **getter methods**:

### MouseEvent Methods
- `int getX()` / `int getY()` — coordinates of mouse.
- `int getClickCount()` — single vs. double-click.
- `int getButton()` — `MouseEvent.BUTTON1`, `BUTTON2`, `BUTTON3`.
- `boolean isShiftDown()`, `isControlDown()`, `isAltDown()` — modifier keys.

### KeyEvent Methods
- `char getKeyChar()` — character typed (only in `keyTyped()`).
- `int getKeyCode()` — virtual key code (e.g., `KeyEvent.VK_ENTER`).
- `String getKeyText(int keyCode)` — human-readable name.
- `boolean isActionKey()` — true for keys without Unicode (F1, arrows).

> [!NOTE]
> **KTU Pitfall:** `getKeyChar()` returns `CHAR_UNDEFINED` inside `keyPressed()` and `keyReleased()`. Use `getKeyCode()` instead for non-character keys.

