# 2. Deep Theoretical Analysis & KTU High-Yield Cheat Sheet

## 2.1 JFrame Class Constructors

`JFrame` provides four overloaded constructors. The **most commonly used** in KTU exam programs is the no-argument constructor.

| Constructor | Description | KTU Frequency |
|---|---|---|
| `JFrame()` | Creates a new, initially invisible window with no title | ★★★★★ |
| `JFrame(String title)` | Creates a new, initially invisible window with the given title | ★★★★★ |
| `JFrame(GraphicsConfiguration gc)` | Creates a window using the specified graphics configuration | ★ |
| `JFrame(String title, GraphicsConfiguration gc)` | Combines title + graphics configuration | ★ |

## 2.2 Essential JFrame Methods — The "Operational Toolkit"

These methods are the **bread-and-butter** of every KTU Swing program. Memorizing them is non-negotiable.

| Method | Signature | Purpose | KTU Frequency |
|---|---|---|---|
| `setTitle(String)` | `void` | Sets the title bar text of the window | ★★★★★ |
| `setSize(int, int)` | `void` | Sets width and height in **pixels** | ★★★★★ |
| `setVisible(boolean)` | `void` | Makes window visible (`true`) or invisible (`false`) | ★★★★★ |
| `setDefaultCloseOperation(int)` | `void` | Defines behavior on window close button click | ★★★★★ |
| `setLayout(LayoutManager)` | `void` | Sets the layout manager of the content pane | ★★★★ |
| `add(Component)` | `Component` | Adds a component to the content pane | ★★★★★ |
| `getContentPane()` | `Container` | Returns the content pane object | ★★★★ |
| `setContentPane(Container)` | `void` | Replaces the default content pane | ★★ |
| `setLocation(int, int)` | `void` | Sets the window's top-left pixel coordinates | ★★★ |
| `setBounds(int, int, int, int)` | `void` | Combines location + size (x, y, w, h) | ★★★★ |
| `setResizable(boolean)` | `void` | Allows/prevents window resizing | ★★★ |
| `pack()` | `void` | Sizes window to fit preferred sizes of components | ★★★ |
| `setIconImage(Image)` | `void` | Sets the window's taskbar/title icon | ★ |
| `getDefaultCloseOperation()` | `int` | Returns the current default close operation | ★★ |

## 2.3 The `WindowConstants` Exit Codes

`JFrame` implements `WindowConstants` which provides these integer constants — **must be memorized** for exams:

| Constant | Value | Behavior on Close |
|---|---|---|
| `DO_NOTHING_ON_CLOSE` | `0` | Ignore the close click (window won't close) |
| `HIDE_ON_CLOSE` | `1` | Hide the window (default for `JDialog`/`JInternalFrame`) |
| `DISPOSE_ON_CLOSE` | `2` | Free window resources after closing |
| `EXIT_ON_CLOSE` | `3` | Terminate the JVM using `System.exit(0)` |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Default Behavior**: If you forget to call `setDefaultCloseOperation()`, the default is `HIDE_ON_CLOSE` — the window disappears but **the program keeps running in the background**. This is a classic KTU exam pitfall!

## 2.4 The Anatomy of a JFrame (Layered Architecture)

`JFrame` is NOT a single flat rectangle. It is a **multi-layered container** — understanding this is critical for KTU board exams.

```
+--------------------------------------------+
|         JFrame (Top-Level Window)          |
|  +--------------------------------------+  |
|  |  Root Pane (JRootPane)               |  |
|  |  +--------------------------------+  |  |
|  |  |  Glass Pane (invisible layer)  |  |  |
|  |  |  +--------------------------+  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  Layered Pane            |  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  +--------------------+  |  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  |  Content Pane      |  |  |  |  <-- You add components HERE
|  |  |  |  |  (default:         |  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  |  BorderLayout)     |  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  +--------------------+  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  +--------------------+  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  |  Menu Bar (optional)|  |  |  |
|  |  |  |  +--------------------+  |  |  |
|  |  |  +--------------------------+  |  |
|  |  +--------------------------------+  |
|  +--------------------------------------+ |
+--------------------------------------------+
```

> [!WARNING]
> **Common Student Mistake**: Calling `add()` directly on a `JFrame` from JDK 5+ is technically still allowed (it delegates to the content pane) but is considered **bad practice** in KTU board evaluation. Always use `getContentPane().add()` or set the content pane explicitly.

