### 5.2 Part B — Long Answer Questions (14 Marks Each)

---

**Q.A. [KTU University Exam – June 2024] — (a) 7 Marks, (b) 7 Marks**

**(a)** Explain the five-step procedure to connect a Java program to a MySQL database using JDBC. Write a Java program to **insert** a new record into a table `employee(emp_id, name, salary)` using `PreparedStatement`.

**(b)** Write a complete Java program to **retrieve and display** all records from the `employee` table whose salary is greater than ₹30,000. Use `PreparedStatement` and `try-with-resources`. Also explain the role of `ResultSet.next()`.

**Model Solution:**

**Part (a) — Connection + INSERT (7 Marks):**

*Five Steps:*

1. **Load the Driver:** `Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");`
2. **Establish Connection:** `DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/companydb", "root", "password");`
3. **Create PreparedStatement:** `con.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO employee(emp_id, name, salary) VALUES (?, ?, ?)");`
4. **Set Parameters and Execute:** `pst.setInt(1, id); ... pst.executeUpdate();`
5. **Close Connection:** Automatically via try-with-resources.

```java
import java.sql.*;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class InsertEmployee {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url  = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/companydb";
        String user = "root";
        String pass = "password";

        try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);
             Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in)) {

            System.out.print("Enter ID, Name, Salary: ");
            int id = sc.nextInt();
            String name = sc.next();
            double sal = sc.nextDouble();

            String sql = "INSERT INTO employee(emp_id, name, salary) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";
            try (PreparedStatement pst = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {
                pst.setInt(1, id);
                pst.setString(2, name);
                pst.setDouble(3, sal);
                int rows = pst.executeUpdate();
                System.out.println(rows + " record inserted.");
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

*(2 marks for 5 steps + 5 marks for complete code = 7 marks.)*

**Part (b) — SELECT with WHERE clause (7 Marks):**

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class HighSalaryEmployees {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/companydb";
        String user = "root";
        String pass = "password";
        double threshold = 30000;

        String sql = "SELECT emp_id, name, salary FROM employee WHERE salary > ?";

        try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);
             PreparedStatement pst = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            pst.setDouble(1, threshold);

            try (ResultSet rs = pst.executeQuery(sql)) {
                System.out.printf("%-10s %-20s %-10s%n", "ID", "Name", "Salary");
                System.out.println("------------------------------------------");
                while (rs.next()) {
                    int id    = rs.getInt("emp_id");
                    String n  = rs.getString("name");
                    double s  = rs.getDouble("salary");
                    System.out.printf("%-10d %-20s %-10.2f%n", id, n, s);
                }
            }
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

**Role of `ResultSet.next()` (2 marks within the 7):**
The `next()` method moves the cursor to the next row in the result set and returns `true` if a row exists, or `false` when no more rows remain. It is essential for iterating through the result set inside a `while` loop. Initially, the cursor is positioned *before* the first row, so the first call to `next()` retrieves the first record.

*(5 marks code + 2 marks explanation = 7 marks.)*

---

**Q.B. [KTU University Exam – Dec 2023 — Alternative Choice] — (a) 7 Marks, (b) 7 Marks**

**(a)** With a neat diagram, explain the JDBC architecture. Differentiate between `Statement` and `PreparedStatement`.

**(b)** Write a Java program using JDBC to **update** the salary of all employees in the `employee` table by 10% who belong to department `'CSE'`. Then write a separate program to **delete** employees whose salary is less than ₹15,000. Use `try-with-resources` and `PreparedStatement`.

**Model Solution:**

**Part (a) — JDBC Architecture + Comparison (7 Marks):**

JDBC Architecture consists of the following layers (refer to the Mermaid diagram in Section 4.1 of these notes):

- **Java Application Layer:** The user program that calls JDBC API methods.
- **JDBC API Layer:** The `java.sql` package providing interfaces such as `Connection`, `Statement`, `ResultSet`.
- **Driver Manager Layer:** Selects the appropriate database driver from registered drivers.
- **JDBC Driver Layer:** Vendor-specific implementation that translates JDBC calls into database-specific protocol.
- **Database Layer:** The actual RDBMS (MySQL, Oracle, etc.) that processes the SQL.

**Difference Table (3 marks):**

| Feature | `Statement` | `PreparedStatement` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| SQL Type | Static | Parameterized |
| Pre-compiled | No | Yes |
| SQL Injection | Vulnerable | Safe |
| Performance | Slower for repeated use | Faster for repeated use |

*(2 marks diagram + 2 marks difference + 3 marks explanation = 7 marks.)*

**Part (b) — UPDATE + DELETE (7 Marks):**

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class EmployeeMaintenance {
    static final String URL  = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/companydb";
    static final String USER = "root";
    static final String PASS = "password";

    public static void updateSalaries() {
        String sql = "UPDATE employee SET salary = salary * 1.10 WHERE department = ?";
        try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(URL, USER, PASS);
             PreparedStatement pst = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {
            pst.setString(1, "CSE");
            int n = pst.executeUpdate();
            System.out.println(n + " CSE employee salaries updated by 10%.");
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void deleteLowSalary() {
        String sql = "DELETE FROM employee WHERE salary < ?";
        try (Connection con = DriverManager.getConnection(URL, USER, PASS);
             PreparedStatement pst = con.prepareStatement(sql)) {
            pst.setDouble(1, 15000);
            int n = pst.executeUpdate();
            System.out.println(n + " employees deleted (salary < 15000).");
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        updateSalaries();
        deleteLowSalary();
    }
}
```

*(3.5 marks for UPDATE code + 3.5 marks for DELETE code = 7 marks.)*

---

> [!WARNING]
> **KTU Examiner's Valuation Pitfalls — Lose-Mark Callouts:**
> 1. **Using `executeQuery()` for `INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE`** → *Zero marks for the execution part.* Always check your method.
> 2. **Forgetting to close `Connection` / `Statement`** → Deduct 1 mark; KTU now expects `try-with-resources`.
> 3. **Hard-coding credentials inside the program** is acceptable in labs but never use `Class.forName()` errors silently — always print the stack trace or rethrow.
> 4. **Wrong parameter index in `setXxx()`** (e.g., starting from 0 instead of 1) — JDBC uses **1-based indexing**.
> 5. **Not handling `SQLException`** is an *unchecked-throws* error — your code will not compile. Always wrap in `try-catch` or declare `throws`.

